 @Ceeby1234  I had to buy a new card only shortly before the 20 series was officially announced (yes I knew it was coming). My old 980 died on me and I didn't want to go a month or 2 waiting for AIB cards so I went and got a 1080 for $500. But in the end the 20 series was disappointing anyways. 


I was considering getting a 2080 ti before it launched, until I saw the price jump that is. No way in hell am I paying an extra $500 for the generational upgrade to the 1080 ti. That's a scam. And the 2080 being sold for the same price as the 1080 ti and with the same performance only confounds this. At these prices there is no way to justify the tiny improvement in performance. 


RTX is cool and all but we have yet to have actual fully ray traced games. Considering the 2080 ti could barely run Quake 2 at 1080p fully ray traced only proves it wasn't ready. No doubt RT is the future, but not sure the 20 series is able to justify it given how poorly it works in it's current implementations.

 @AVerySillySausage  Holy shit what a paragraph of 0 educated thoughts on the topic.
If you buy a 2080ti, you're an enthusiast, if you weren't buying the ti's before hand, you never will buy the next one.

NEVER expect a huge performance leap from series to series, science literally tells us that performance will very soon stagnate, as it already is. For example performance gains stop at 32~ cpu cores, and the same goes for gpus, it stops somewhere. And when it stops, to increase performance by the same amount on the next product, you require 2x or even 3x the amount of resources, this has been shown with every gpu, both amd and nvidia. THAT IS LITERALLY how engineering works..........

