 @3rd.world.eliteAJ performance hasnt stagnated. Seriously? Consider this...
 If you look at the 5700xt, which IS a polaris replacement by looking at the die. It's almost exactly the same size and uses gddr memory. That card compared to polaris is an almost 100% generational leap. The issue is that nvidia ruined the gpu price market due to no real competition on by AMD. That's why the 5700xt cost $400. They could not justify a 80% price increase if they called it a RX690. Nvidia has stagnated and will continue to since theyve committed to putting in RT and tensor cores in their chips. They cannot reduce their die size for at least the next 2 card generations due to this. They cant just take those features out... If they remove the rt and tensors after talking them up as the greatest thing, they will look utterly incompetent to their shareholders. Not to mention all the early adopters feeling completely burned for buying into it just to be not supported anymore. They HAVE to use them for at least the next 2 generations now..
I read the patent for AMDs ray tracing that is coming out on consoles and it is NOTHING like Nvidia's implementation of it... programmers will not want to have to write a new set of code just so PC users with nvidia can have the features. Nvidia can pay the programmers but that cost will just be passed onto the consumer... im no fannboi, I have an Nvidia card, but they literally have painted themselves into a corner....
